Toolchain: sqlite3 3.53.4 2026-07-24; python3 (stdlib sqlite3) for seeding
Commit:    5de978dccb5d9fb01903df9acc3d8a8179078707 (tag 0.27.2) + Subject 01

## Setup

Fresh database from sql/0001_initial.sql, one user, one target, and 1500
rows inserted into check_results with checked_at = 2020-01-01 (all
eligible under any real retention policy — the default check_results
policy is 90 days).

$ sqlite3 db.sqlite < sql/0001_initial.sql
$ # seed: 1 user, 1 target, 1500 check_results rows at checked_at='2020-01-01T00:00:00Z'
$ sqlite3 db.sqlite "SELECT count(*) FROM check_results;"
1500

---

## T-04 / T-05 — more than one batch eligible: archived count vs deleted count

MUST FAIL FIRST. Cutoff = 2026-01-01T00:00:00Z (all 1500 rows eligible).

### Archive step — exact SQL from archive_old_records (LIMIT 1000, hard-coded)

$ sqlite3 db.sqlite "SELECT count(*) FROM (SELECT * FROM check_results WHERE checked_at < '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z' LIMIT 1000);"
1000

### Delete step — exact SQL from run_cleanup (no LIMIT)

$ sqlite3 db.sqlite "DELETE FROM check_results WHERE checked_at < '2026-01-01T00:00:00Z';"
$ sqlite3 db.sqlite "SELECT count(*) FROM check_results;"
0

# exit code: 0 for both statements — no error is raised anywhere. The
# defect is silent: archived_count (1000) != deleted_count (1500).
# 500 records were deleted with no archive copy ever written for them.
# This is G-20 exactly as described, and is not a hypothetical scale
# concern — 1500 rows accumulates in roughly 25 hours at a 1-minute
# check interval across a few hundred targets with a default 90-day
# check_results retention policy.

---

## T-06 — forced archive failure → zero records deleted, failure surfaced

NOT RUN. `archive_old_records`'s R2 `.put(...).execute().await?` and the
subsequent unconditional `DELETE` in `run_cleanup` are sequenced with
`?`-propagation, so by inspection a failing archive already aborts
before the delete statement for that table is reached in the *current*
code. Verifying this requires fault-injecting an R2 write inside a real
Worker/D1 environment, which is not available here. The Subject 02 fix
must preserve this ordering per-batch (archive before delete, `?`
between them) rather than assume it is automatically safe once
batching is introduced — see the Build section.

## T-07 — aborted pass then successful pass → each record archived and deleted exactly once

NOT RUN for the same reason as T-06 — requires injecting a fault
mid-pass against a real Worker runtime. The post-fix design argument
(each batch is deleted immediately after being archived, so an aborted
pass leaves only not-yet-processed rows eligible for the next run) is
recorded in requirements.md and the Subject 02 completion notes; it is
a property of the control flow, not something exercised here.

